                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "I want to pre-face this review with this: Graveyard Keeper is not a bad game, but its a bad game for me. I played on Steam deck, and found the game did not run very smoothly at all, instead having these weird tears that I associate with the Pokemon Silver and moving between Kanto and Johto in Mt. Silver. It didn't hamper gameplay, but it was certainly odd and may effect your gaming experience if you choose to play on Steam deck\r\n\r\nI've always been drawn to Graveyard Keeper as a game since it was announced. I've seen my friends play it, and it looked like a lot of fun. And on sale, it was a bargain to pick up. Yet, when I finally got around to playing it, I forgot a key difference between myself and my friends:\r\nThey love number go up games, and; they love a game where you do a thing to make thing better at doing a thing. And Graveyard Keeper fills that kind of itch in a game.\r\n\r\nWhat are its positives for me? \r\nIts got a pretty good sense of humour, and I really enjoy its level system; getting red, blue, and green points which can be spent to upgrade your character. This means you get to do more over time in a natural way, and once you understand how to do certain things (which are all quite clear how to do without a wikipedia open next to you), its an easy enough game to do the things you need to do. Its just...there's a lot to do.\r\n\r\nWhen I say there's a lot to do...you have to talk to the astrologist on Moon day, talk to the Inquisitor on Male day, hold a sermon on Sun day, maintain your supplies of iron, stone, marble, wood, etc., to ensure that you're able to upgrade your church to get more money to ensure that you can complete a quest which will allow you to upgrade your church (hence my comment about number go up and do the thing to get better at the thing earlier).\r\n\r\nWhat are its negatives for me?\r\nGeneric soundtrack, lots of things to remember and keep on top of, slow walking (yes, there's an item to let you teleport, doesn't change this issue), days go by extremely fast to the point everything's a rush to get what you need done, and a lot of generic NPCs. So where it shines best, which is the humour and tech tree, it doesn't do enough for me to think its a game I want to continue playing.\r\n\r\nWho would I recommend this to?\r\nThese may feel like wild ones to throw out there, but people who enjoy games like Monster Hunter, Factorio, Cookie Clicker, and Vampire Survivors will likely enjoy this game due to the core gameplay loop being similar.\r\nI believe those who enjoy farming games may not mesh with the humour necessarily depending on what they enjoy in those farming games, but I will suggest Stardew Valley due to some of the humour in that game and fundamentally being very similar.\r\n \r\nWho would I not recommend this to?\r\nIf you don't like games that are number get bigger like Disgaea or any other Nippon Ichi Software major titles, then you'll be unlikely to enjoy this game.\r\nIf you don't like repetitive tasks, you will be unlikely to enjoy Graveyard Keeper.\r\nIf you get overwhelmed or analysis paralysis deciding what you need to do in the most effective way to ensure the best performance out of your game (i.e. min/maxing), then I'd stay away from Graveyard Keeper.\r\n\r\nI don't have any wild, out there recommendations for this Graveyard Keeper because I really think the game is a particular kind of game that either you love or hate.\r\n\r\nIn summary, Graveyard Keeper is a good edition to the game library for any fan of incremental games, and games that focus on automation and min/maxing your time and build. It has good humour with an okay story, but may fail to capture you if you don't like tedium in your game play.",
